    "narrative": "Our primary engine today is **Corbin Carroll**, who anchors the lineup with an elite 81.08 score against V\u00e1squez. He benefits from a favorable 4.80 xERA matchup and a prime spot in the heart of the order, making him our highest-floor asset for steals and counting stats. We are also leaning heavily on the high-efficiency trio of **Brice Turang** (66.27), **Isaac Paredes** (63.82), and **James Wood** (61.74). **Brice Turang** is particularly intriguing; his score is boosted by a 4% efficiency premium due to his high stolen base potential against a slower pitcher, perfectly aligning with our organizational priority of hunting speed. Meanwhile, **Isaac Paredes** capitalizes on a strong park factor and a favorable platoon advantage, and **James Wood** remains a must-start despite the presence of a tough catcher, as his elite .404 xwOBA and top-of-the-order volume override the situational friction.\n\nThe decision to bench **Vinnie Pasquantino** (41.02) was the most contentious call of the morning. While he remains a steady professional hitter, his matchup against the southpaw Messick forces a significant downward adjustment due to the dynamic platoon penalty. He simply could not clear the bar set by **Josh Naylor** (58.11), whose platoon disadvantage is mitigated by a more favorable overall matchup and higher projected run-production upside. We are keeping a close eye on **Jose Caballero** (51.12), as his lineup placement is currently speculative; if he slides lower than the ninth spot or if the wind intensity increases, his efficiency could dip further into the marginal tier. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Moises Ballesteros** (45.66) and **Trevor Story** (44.30), both of whom are facing difficult SPs in Gilbert and Imanaga, respectively, leaving them as the most likely candidates to be subbed out if we need to prioritize game-cap preservation later in the day.",
